    {
      "id": "claim_a5fd049e2b204eca98345b5554ec8f9e",
      "statement": "ConsentsGate wraps the router output in App.tsx, above AuthGuard. A blocked user has no session (the /users/me session check is refused by the Guards rule), so AuthGuard would bounce them to the login page and they would never see an acceptance screen. The overlay is user experience, not enforcement — removing it with devtools does not bypass the server rule.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://src/App.tsx#L55-L57",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:558e2563dc0ddb6994fedc76258ea67c43f5c374cd9cc494ed7814f29fcf342e:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://src/ConsentsGate.tsx#L6-L17",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:c352e9546d9ac4dfa257d713d6cf7a0c21a95834886f0aedb6df221932cb91c0:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_4ae67f02200d41c580d788eaee469e01",
      "statement": "When the user clicks 'I accept' in the ConsentsGate overlay, the app calls auth.acceptConsents() which PATCHes /users with {\"consents\": []}. The permission's mergeRequest stamps the current TOS_VERSION and PP_VERSION onto latestConsents and pushes to the consents history array server-side — the client states nothing about which versions are being accepted. After the PATCH, auth.checkSession() fetches a fresh JWT via /token?renew=true then re-reads /users/me, because a JWT is a snapshot and without a fresh one the rule keeps blocking the user for the life of the old token.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://src/ConsentsGate.tsx#L33-L51",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:36821e11be376177dcd4b12efe71f864ef22254c37efae989582b05cea96fbf5:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://ulabase.setup.consents.ts#L207-L222",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:e62c9016a0a7d66ac5836306aad21a68726ec03607573ebfc23076130c4f163e:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_6b8ee26e366740e9ae6ce74c1ab48519",
      "statement": "TOS_VERSION and PP_VERSION are defined once in ulabase.setup.consents.ts and derived into the permission's mergeRequest and the rule's condition. Publishing new terms means editing two strings and re-running setup; every user meets the acceptance form again on their next request. The 'Version 2026-07-01' line at the top of public/terms.html and public/privacy.html is the third place and the only one kept in step by hand — all three must match or users accept one version while the server records another.",
      "evidence": [
