    {
      "id": "claim_3c1f76b9e68c4cd9a7586ed0a74994b5",
      "statement": "After OAuth redirect, the access token arrives in the URL fragment (#access_token=...). consumeFragmentToken() in App.tsx reads window.location.hash, extracts the token, calls setToken() and scheduleRefresh(), also checks for ?flow=signup to set the justSignedUp flag, and cleans the URL with history.replaceState. This runs once on mount before any route renders.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://src/App.tsx#L10-L32",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:8bdf5a6e8f2537e40e4711ac165e6796ca60ad417b5de47dba4f5fb781e123a5:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_a8a4fc654026421fa8cd85aa2fffe087",
      "statement": "auth.api(path) is the authenticated fetch wrapper for application data collections. It attaches the bearer token automatically, rejects non-2xx responses as ApiError({status, message}), and accepts a path (not a full URL). A plain fetch to the same URL is unauthenticated and the service answers 401. A 451 from the consents rule or a 403 from an ACL is something you branch on rather than parse.",
